WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.551
A Go service usually sits between many
systems.

2
00:00:02.631 --> 00:00:05.451
It may receive calls from clients,
gateways, internal services,

3
00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:07.890
background jobs, administrative tools, or
webhooks.

4
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.457
It may call databases, caches, queues,
identity providers, logging platforms,

5
00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:13.896
object stores, cloud APIs, and third-party
services.

6
00:00:13.976 --> 00:00:16.540
Each connection is a boundary where
assumptions can break.

7
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.210
A boundary is not only a network edge.

8
00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.572
A message queue is a boundary.

9
00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:20.831
A file import is a boundary.

10
00:00:20.911 --> 00:00:22.501
A configuration value is a boundary.

11
00:00:22.581 --> 00:00:26.068
A database record written by an older
version of the service can also behave like a

12
00:00:26.148 --> 00:00:29.019
boundary because its shape and meaning may
not match current code.

13
00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:31.868
Boundaries are where data and authority
move between contexts.

14
00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:35.076
Internal traffic should not automatically
be treated as fully trusted.

15
00:00:35.156 --> 00:00:38.284
Internal callers can be misconfigured,
outdated, over-permissioned,

16
00:00:38.364 --> 00:00:40.774
or compromised through ordinary
operational failure.

17
00:00:40.854 --> 00:00:44.444
A secure architecture avoids assuming that
private network location alone proves

18
00:00:44.524 --> 00:00:44.883
safety.

19
00:00:44.963 --> 00:00:48.039
It still validates inputs, authenticates
callers where appropriate,

20
00:00:48.119 --> 00:00:50.376
and enforces authorization for protected
actions.

21
00:00:50.456 --> 00:00:52.968
Trust zones help engineers decide where
controls belong.

22
00:00:53.048 --> 00:00:56.228
At one boundary, the service may need
request validation and rate limits.

23
00:00:56.308 --> 00:00:59.025
At another, it may need service identity,
scoped credentials,

24
00:00:59.105 --> 00:01:00.387
or careful output filtering.

25
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:04.006
At another, it may need audit events
because administrative actions or sensitive

26
00:01:04.086 --> 00:01:05.060
records are involved.

27
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.524
The control should match the trust
relationship and the risk of the operation.

28
00:01:08.604 --> 00:01:11.425
A good architecture makes boundaries
visible in code structure,

29
00:01:11.505 --> 00:01:14.222
configuration, deployment, and operational
documentation.

30
00:01:14.302 --> 00:01:16.918
Handlers, middleware, service layers,
repository layers,

31
00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:19.972
and background workers should make it
clear where input is accepted,

32
00:01:20.052 --> 00:01:22.462
where it is normalized, where permissions
are checked,

33
00:01:22.542 --> 00:01:23.875
and where side effects happen.

34
00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:27.288
Clear boundaries reduce confusion during
maintenance, incident response,

35
00:01:27.368 --> 00:01:28.445
and future feature work.

36
00:01:28.525 --> 00:01:32.166
When a team can quickly identify which
boundary failed or which assumption changed,

37
00:01:32.246 --> 00:01:35.220
it can fix the design instead of patching
symptoms in unrelated code.
